# Product and machine test separation: issue 170

## Result

The product no longer enumerates unrelated machine worktrees as part of
correctness. The operational concern is covered by the existing disk steward's
ownership-reconciliation and free-space modules, with additional isolated
controls committed separately in private `KatsuJinCode/diskSteward` at
`98e6abacc5ea5bf85d8e6a11117bb398cc2a540c`.

The product change retires the machine test, its historical keep list and its
historical machine failure transcript from the current tree. Their exact
source/history remains in the committed parent
`dd8ec464957e9324a45af7d00f60973b2beb484d`; each on-disk file was compared with
that committed copy before removal. No unrelated worktree or folder is removed.

`tools/test-gates.mjs` no longer implicitly classifies `tests/hygiene/` as
product correctness. An unclassified test makes every gate refuse inventory
validation; it is not silently skipped. The two-case portable scope regression
pins this refusal and preserves classification of actual rendering, freshness,
acceptance, deployment and tooling checks. No Vitest exclusion or skip was
added, and no product behavior assertion was removed.

## Reproduction and negative controls

The unchanged original test was executed first. It failed on external machine
state: four unrelated lane directories missing from an old keep/coordination
list and an unavailable historical repository path. The failure did not reach
any Decko data, request or render behavior. The original frozen release-owner
failure receipt remains untouched.

The new scope test was red before the classification change: the machine test
was wrongly returned as `correctness`. After changing classification but before
retiring the old file, the actual inventory CLI failed explicitly with that
file's `no declared test gate` error. This preserved negative control verifies
that the correction does not turn a discovered test into a silent omission.

After retirement, inventory validation passes for all 1,352 discovered tests,
including 137 using Node's test runner. A selected local execution passes 30
tests across the new scope file, existing render-engine controls and existing
freshness-stamp controls, with zero skips or failures. The initial typecheck
failed TS7016 at the new test's JavaScript-module import; a subsequent successful
shell command masked its exit, so the original passing claim was incorrect.
Issue 174 supplies the precise module declaration and a separately checked
native compiler-exit receipt. The two-file platform scan reports zero command landmines; whitespace checks
pass. This is focused verification, not a full correctness/release invocation.

## Effective machine owner

The existing machine path is create-worktree event -> worktree sweep -> project
directory reconciliation. Unknown directories receive protective review custody;
links, live owners, existing custody and unknown observations retain their
protection. The existing free-space authority is the machine's configured
1 GiB remaining after a write. The old population and disallowed-size budgets
are retired; neither is raised or replaced by a larger cap.

The machine commit changes only two existing operational test files and its
own report. It changes no production tool, policy, approval/deletion guard,
settings, kill switch, trigger or schedule. Its focused reconciliation,
free-space and preservation execution passes 33 tests and two subtests with
zero skips/failures. Added fixtures cover the four historical names, sixteen
owned/reviewed directories across the retired population limit, and exact
after-write boundary readings below/at/above 1 GiB. All fixture contents remain;
removal is zero. A separate inert in-memory no-custody replacement produces
the expected `0 != 4` assertion failure. No live cleanup or reconciliation was
applied. Independent reviewer acceptance remains required.

A broader pre-existing no-disk-admission test separately failed two assertions
and skipped one: fixture/error-handler strings are treated as active policy,
and the default floor is expected to be literal zero instead of the current
named constant. That combined invocation reported two failed, 37 passed, one
skipped and nine subtests; it is not acceptance. System issue
`rel-aecc4977-473c-4e89-8765-4bd80b85c94c` records this mismatch. Its protected
policy and guard files are untouched. The focused passing result above does
not claim that this broader operational suite is repaired.

